WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.310
In this video, you create a two gigabyte volume and hot-plug it into a running virtual machine, adding storage without any downtime.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:15.460
First, create the volume. On the Volumes page, select Create.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.420
Choose the namespace of the virtual machine.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.510
Name the volume.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:27.280
Keep the source set to New, an empty volume, and keep the default storage class.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.000
Set the size to two gigabytes.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.950
Select Create.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:39.040
On the Virtual Machines page, open the action menu of the running virtual machine.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.760
Select Add Volume.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.780
Enter a name for the disk inside the virtual machine.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.120
Choose the volume you created.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.590
Select Apply.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.430
Open the virtual machine to check the result.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.950
It is still running. Nothing was restarted.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.860
Select the Volumes tab.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:09.970
Below the root disk, the new two gigabyte volume named data is attached and ready.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:16.558
The volume is attached to the running virtual machine. Next, you expand a disk.
